08:46:31  INFO  [msg ← Uni] KP response to your acm.org ticket — definitive diagnosis (CANNOT, pending admin action):

ROOT CAUSE (primary): The upstream recursive resolver at 4.2.2.1 (AS1) is configured with a static DNS override:
    dnsmasq --address=/acm.org/10.0.0.99
This hijacks acm.org -> 10.0.0.99 (RFC1918 private space). It is NOT a real ACM address. The legitimate ACM addresses (learned by AS1 via AS2) are 198.82.0.1 / 137.54.0.1 / 192.107.102.1. Your browser/ping is being sent to an unroutable private IP, which is why traffic fails.

SECONDARY (the loop you saw): 10.0.0.99 is globally unroutable. AS1's default points at AS2 (peer), and AS2 had no specific route either, so packets bounced between them — the classic TTL-exceeded loop. The symptom has since changed shape on its own: AS2 is currently dropping with ICMP Net Unreachable / silent drops instead of looping, but the underlying RFC1918-leaks-to-default issue still exists as a hygiene item.

FIX STATUS: CANNOT (pending admin action).
- AS1 has identified the misconfiguration and proposed removing the `--address=/acm.org/10.0.0.99` override.
- However, that override appears to be a deliberate policy/security decision (explicit CLI flag), not an accident. AS1 will NOT unilaterally remove it. Administrators at AS1 have been notified and approval is pending.
- Secondary RFC1918 routing-hygiene fix also requires AS1<->AS2 coordination + admin sign-off.

WORKAROUND for you in the meantime: if you trust the route, you can reach ACM directly by IP (e.g. https://198.82.0.1 / 137.54.0.1 / 192.107.102.1), bypassing the hijacked DNS answer. Use a different resolver only with your admin's blessing.

I'll send a corrected update if/when AS1's admins act on it.
